# Creating a New Project

Before starting, make sure the [Prerequisites](prerequisites.md) are in place.

1. Ensure you have [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/getting-started.html) installed on your workstation (Linux/macOS/Windows are all supported).
1. Clone this repository and open it in your terminal
1. Run the following to install needed dependencies to use the template:

    ```bash
    mise trust
    mise install
    ```

1. Run the following to create the template, making sure to set the path at the end as appropriate

    ```bash
    mise x "--" copier copy --trust {% if using_github %}https://github.com/{{ github_repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}{% elif using_azdo %}git+https://dev.azure.com/{{ azdo_org }}/{{ azdo_project_encoded }}/_git/{{ repo_name }}{% endif %} ~/local-repo-path-here
    ```

See [Template Questions](../reference/template-questions.md) for what each prompt during that step controls.

## After Creation

What's left to do depends on your `repo_setup_actions` answer and your platform.

=== "Create Repo"

    === "GitHub"

        The copy step has already:

        - Created the remote repo, pushed an initial commit to `main`, and opened the first
          release PR (proposing `0.1.0`, or `1.0.0` if `lifecycle` is `Stable`)
        - Allowed workflows to open pull requests
        - Set up GitHub Pages, if `zensical_target` is `GitHub Pages`
        - Enabled CodeQL code scanning and private vulnerability reporting, if the repo is public
        - Opened a **Template Setup Checklist** issue

        Then finish setup:

        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the secrets CI needs; see [Secrets and
           Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the setup checklist issue.
        1. If you haven't installed the [Settings GitHub App](https://github.com/apps/settings),
           apply the settings in [Manual Repo Settings](../reference/manual-repo-settings.md).
        1. Merge the first release PR once PR Validation passes; see [Releasing](../maintaining/releasing.md).

    === "Azure DevOps"

        The copy step has already:

        - Created the remote repo, pushed an initial commit to `main`, and opened the first
          release PR (proposing `0.1.0`, or `1.0.0` if `lifecycle` is `Stable`)
        - Registered a pipeline for each file under `.azurepipelines/`
        - Required the PR Validation pipeline to pass before a PR to `main` can complete
        - Printed the setup checklist at the end of the run

        Then finish setup:

        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the pipeline variables CI needs; see
           [Secrets and Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the printed setup checklist.
        1. Merge the first release PR once PR Validation passes; see [Releasing](../maintaining/releasing.md).

=== "Set Repo Rules"

    === "GitHub"

        The copy step left repo creation and Git to you, but has already:

        - Allowed workflows to open pull requests
        - Set up GitHub Pages, if `zensical_target` is `GitHub Pages`
        - Enabled CodeQL code scanning and private vulnerability reporting, if the repo is public
        - Opened a **Template Setup Checklist** issue

        Then finish setup:

        1. Commit the generated files and merge them to `main`; the first push to `main` opens
           the first release PR.
        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the secrets CI needs; see [Secrets and
           Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the setup checklist issue.
        1. If you haven't installed the [Settings GitHub App](https://github.com/apps/settings),
           apply the settings in [Manual Repo Settings](../reference/manual-repo-settings.md).

    === "Azure DevOps"

        The copy step left repo creation and Git to you, but has already:

        - Registered a pipeline for each file under `.azurepipelines/`
        - Required the PR Validation pipeline to pass before a PR to `main` can complete
        - Printed the setup checklist at the end of the run

        Then finish setup:

        1. Commit the generated files and merge them to `main`; the first push to `main` opens
           the first release PR.
        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the pipeline variables CI needs; see
           [Secrets and Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the printed setup checklist.

=== "None"

    === "GitHub"

        The copy step only generated files and printed the setup checklist. Then finish setup:

        1. Create the remote repo, then commit and push the generated files to `main`; that
           first push opens the first release PR.
        1. Apply the settings in [Manual Repo Settings](../reference/manual-repo-settings.md), or
           install the [Settings GitHub App](https://github.com/apps/settings) to sync them from
           `.github/settings.yml`.
        1. Under **Settings > Actions > General**, enable **Allow GitHub Actions to create and
           approve pull requests**.
        1. If `zensical_target` is `GitHub Pages`, set **Settings > Pages > Source** to
           **GitHub Actions**.
        1. If the repo is public, enable CodeQL's default setup and private vulnerability
           reporting under **Settings > Advanced Security**.
        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the secrets CI needs; see [Secrets and
           Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the printed setup checklist.

    === "Azure DevOps"

        The copy step only generated files and printed the setup checklist. Then finish setup:

        1. Create the remote repo, then commit and push the generated files to `main`.
        1. Register the pipelines and the PR Validation branch policy as described in [Manual
           Repo Settings](../reference/manual-repo-settings.md); the first release PR opens once
           the release pipeline exists and runs on `main`.
        1. Run `mise run provision-secrets` to set the pipeline variables CI needs; see
           [Secrets and Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md).
        1. Work through the printed setup checklist.
